Build the arm64 User-mode Linux host code against current glibc headers

The kernel's compiler attributes define __alloc_size__ as a macro. Host-side
User-mode Linux files include that definition before glibc's sys/types.h,
whose own __alloc_size__ attribute then expands to the kernel macro and
fails to compile. The shared user header drops the kernel macro before it
includes the host headers.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a kernel diff under
consonance/harmony-linux/linux/patches/ per the repository's kernel-patch
exception.

---
--- a/arch/um/include/shared/user.h
+++ b/arch/um/include/shared/user.h
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 #include <linux/types.h>
 #else
 #include <stddef.h>
+#undef __alloc_size__
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #endif
 
